A||||7||88formula||Crane Comp-u-Cam||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||04-23-2000||10:12 PM||||I want to try the Crane Comp-U-Cane for my 2.8. The Cam I would like to use is the one that the fiero store sells. It has 204 degrees of duration @ .050 inch intake, 216 exhaust and .423 lift intake, .454 exhaust. I have some questions for those of you that have this cam. <BR>(1) Do you loose the low-end torque that the stock cam provides? <BR>(2) How does this cam effect your fuel economy compared to the stock cam?<P>Thanks, you guys are a great help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||04-24-2000||04:42 PM||||Does anyone know? Please.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||04-24-2000||05:59 PM||||88,  I cannot comment on the Crane Cam, but I just put in the GM Performance Cam which is just a little milder than what you described.  I have not had my car dyno'd so I can only describe what I have felt.  Keep in mind I went from a V6 with 190K miles on it and a bad exhaust leak to a rebuild with new cam and cleaned out exhaust manifolds.<P>The torque on the low end feels more or less the same as before.  It definitely develops more power in the mid-range than before and it uses more gas.  I only have 600 miles on the rebuild and am getting 16 mpg around town where I previously got 18 mpg.  Only today did I get some serious highway miles on it, but I don't think it will be much better than 20 mpg, where I was getting 22-24 before.  Keep in mind that the engine is not even really out of it's break-in period so my mileage may actually improve over time.<P>I like the cam.  Gives me a good exhaust tone (no cat) but isn't annoying and the idle is not much different than before.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||04-24-2000||07:42 PM||||Thanks mshill, I appreciate your reply. I think you are right about the lower mileage attributed to the new engine. I have done stock rebuilds for other people (2.5 Pontiac four in a 91 Grand Am for my brother is one) and the same thing happens to them. The mileage is dismal for the first 1000 miles and by 5000 miles it is back to normal.<P>I don’t have much experience with high performance rebuilds or anything to do with high performance but I am learning. I used to think that performance or sports cars were a waist of money until I drove one and since then I have be hooked.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ezsurf||04-24-2000||10:06 PM||||88, the comp-u-cams available for the 2.8 are the 2020 and the 2030.  Duration for the 2020 @ .050 is 198/204; and for the 2030 204/264 @ .050.  I can't say that I recoginze the numbers you posted for the cam. It may be a different one that I am not familiar with. I have the 2020 in my 2.8. I have only driven it 400 miles since the rebuild, and I haven't had it over 60 mph. The low end torque seems good, but I haven't put it through it's paces yet. The timing was way off during the first tank of fuel. I got 22 mpg. With the timing set correctly, I got 26 mpg. Lower end torque was what I wanted. If you are going to drag race it, you will probably want the 2030. It developes it's power at a higher rpm. I say get the cam that delivers it's power in the rpm range you will be running the engine most of the time. That way you will get the max enjoyment out of it. Hope this helps. - ez||||reg||14
Z||000005||Cooter||04-24-2000||11:45 PM||||While I was looking in the GM performance parts catalog and the footnote in the 60 degree v6 cams page said the flat tappet cams were made by Crane for GM and the lift that was listed matched the compu-cam 2020 and 2030. Food for thought.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||04-25-2000||06:22 PM||||Ezsurf, I got the cam specs wrong. It is the 2030 cam. The RPM range is 1500-4500, which is what I am looking for.<P>The Gm cam on paper is a little more aggressive than the Compucam. Now I have to decide which one. I guess I will go with the cheaper one.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 04-25-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 04-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||spiz||04-25-2000||11:15 PM||||I have GM HO cam, lifters, springs, locks, retainers and hi-volume oil pump all brand new in the boxes if your interested.<P>spiz||||reg||1
